  "question": {
    "text": "<p>One end of a copper rod of uniform cross-section and of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;is kept in contact with ice at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;and the other end with water at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>. If the whole system is insulated from its&nbsp;surroundings,&nbsp;then at what&nbsp;distance from the end of the rod which is in contact with the steam, the temperature&nbsp;should be&nbsp;maintained at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, so that in steady-state the rate of melting of ice be equal to the rate of production of steam? <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>fusion</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cal</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>vaporization</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>540</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cal</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></math></p>\r"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>34</mn><mo>&#xA0;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>",
